  { name: 'Raven Territory', url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'Are ravens territorial?', a: 'Yes — breeding raven pairs defend territories that include the nest site and surrounding foraging areas. Territory defence is active, involving aerial chases, calling, and direct displacement of intruders. However, non-breeding ravens (juveniles and unpaired adults) are not territorial and form communal roosts and feeding flocks. Breeding pairs also tolerate their own offspring in the territory for a period after fledging, before evicting them.' },
  { q: 'How large is a raven territory?', a: 'Raven territory size varies enormously with habitat and food availability. In productive areas — coastal environments, areas near landfills or large carcass resources — territories may be 2–10 km². In lower-quality habitats, territories can be 20–50 km² or larger. In Yellowstone National Park, territory sizes have been estimated at roughly 15–25 km². Home ranges (the total area used, including foraging beyond the defended core) are larger than defended territories.' },
  { q: 'How do ravens defend their territory?', a: 'Ravens defend territories through several mechanisms: (1) active aerial pursuit and displacement of intruders; (2) calling — the deep "gronk" calls and other vocalisations signal occupancy; (3) conspicuous soaring and display flights over the territory boundary; (4) direct confrontation at food sources within the territory. Territory defence is most intense during the breeding season (pre-laying through fledging), and somewhat relaxed in non-breeding periods when birds may forage widely.' },
];
