    <h1>Raven Territory: How Ravens Defend Their Home Range</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Breeding raven pairs defend large territories — typically 2–50+ km² depending on habitat quality — that include the nest site and core foraging areas. Non-breeding ravens are non-territorial and form communal flocks. Territory defence involves aerial pursuits, calling, and display flights. Defence is most intense during the breeding season.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Breeding Territories</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Established breeding pairs hold year-round territories, though defence intensity peaks during the breeding season. Territory boundaries are maintained against other breeding pairs through:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Aerial displacement:</strong> territorial ravens pursue and chase intruders, sometimes with physical contact. Both members of a pair participate in defence.</li>
      <li><strong>Calling:</strong> the deep, resonant "gronk" and other calls signal pair occupancy and deter intrusion.</li>
      <li><strong>Display soaring:</strong> conspicuous soaring and aerial displays over the territory boundary serve as visual signals to neighbours.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Well-established pairs can maintain the same territory for many years — in some cases over a decade. The long pair bond and shared territory knowledge is a significant competitive advantage over unpaired intruders.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Territory Size</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Raven territory size is highly variable and inversely correlated with habitat quality and food availability:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Productive habitats</strong> (coastal, near large carcass resources, agricultural): territories as small as 2–5 km².</li>
      <li><strong>Moderate habitats</strong> (montane forest, managed grassland): 10–25 km² is typical in well-studied populations.</li>
      <li><strong>Marginal habitats</strong> (high Arctic, remote upland): territories may exceed 50 km².</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Home ranges — the total area used for foraging, including excursions beyond the defended core — are substantially larger than territories. Tracking studies have shown ravens from a single territory ranging 50+ km from the nest to exploit temporarily abundant food.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Non-Breeding Ravens: The Floater Population</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Not all ravens hold territories. A substantial proportion of the raven population at any time consists of non-breeding "floaters" — subadult and unpaired adult birds who live in communal roosts and form mobile foraging flocks. These birds exploit patchy, unpredictable food sources that territorial birds cannot monopolise. Floaters are subordinate at carcasses within territorial areas but may recruit large groups that overwhelm a territorial pair's ability to defend a carcass.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <p>Heinrich's (1989) information-centre hypothesis proposes that communal roosts serve as information networks: younger, less experienced birds follow successful foragers from the roost to distant food sources each morning.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Transition: From Floater to Territory Holder</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Young ravens typically spend 2–3 years as floaters before acquiring a territory and mate. Territory acquisition occurs when:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>A territory holder dies, creating a vacancy.</li>
      <li>A territory holder loses its mate; a floater pairs with the survivor.</li>
      <li>A new territory is established in previously unoccupied or low-quality habitat.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Once established, territorial pairs typically remain in the territory for life unless displaced by exceptional circumstances.</p>
  </section>

